edges:
- nothing added :: not any(c.startswith("add") for c in commands) :: run(["MyLinkedList", "get"], [[], [0]])
- deleting node 0 :: any(c.startswith("add") for c in commands) and any(c == "deleteAtIndex" and i == [0] for c, i in zip(commands, inputs)) :: run(["MyLinkedList", "addAtHead", "deleteAtIndex", "get"], [[], [1], [0], [0]])
- adds, and no delete of node 0 :: any(c.startswith("add") for c in commands) and not any(c == "deleteAtIndex" and i == [0] for c, i in zip(commands, inputs)) :: run(["MyLinkedList", "addAtHead", "addAtTail", "get"], [[], [1], [2], [1]])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(n)
complexity_note: Give the time of one call; n is the number of nodes in the list.
why_time: Each call walks at most index links from the dummy, and a valid index is at most n.
why_space: One node for each value in the list, and the dummy.
hint: node_before: start at self.dummy and move to .next index times.
hint: get: if index < 0 or index >= self.size, return −1; otherwise return `self.node_before(index).next.val`.
hint: add_at_index: if index > self.size, return. Otherwise prev = node_before(index), `prev.next = Node(val, prev.next)`, and add 1 to the size.
hint: delete_at_index: if index is out of range, return. Otherwise prev = node_before(index), `prev.next = prev.next.next`, and take 1 off the size.
right: The dummy gives position 0 a node before it, so node_before works for every index, and inserting and deleting are each one link change after it, with no special case for the head.
wrong: Keep a dummy and a size. Walk index links from the dummy to the node before position index, then insert or delete after it, checking the index against the size first.

=== task: code medium
title: Remove Duplicates from Sorted List II
leetcode: 82 | Remove Duplicates from Sorted List II | remove-duplicates-from-sorted-list-ii
patterns: prevcur + dummy
prompt: The values of a linked list are sorted from smallest to biggest. Write `delete_duplicates(head)` that deletes **every** node whose value appears more than once, leaving only the values that appear exactly once, and returns the head.

Equal values sit next to each other in a sorted list. Walk with prev, the last node kept, and cur. Start prev on a dummy, because the head itself may go. If cur.next holds the same value as cur, move cur to the last node of that run of equal values, then link prev past the whole run: `prev.next = cur.next`. Otherwise cur is kept, so prev moves on to it. Either way, cur then moves on.

**Example 1:** `run([1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5])` returns `[1, 2, 5]`.

**Example 2:** `run([1, 1, 1, 2, 3])` returns `[2, 3]`: the run of 1s at the front goes, so the head changes.

**Constraints:** the list has 0 to 300 nodes, sorted, and −100 ≤ every value ≤ 100.
starter:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def delete_duplicates(head):
    dummy = Node(0, head)
    prev = dummy
    cur = head
    # your code here
    return dummy.next


def run(values):
    return to_list(delete_duplicates(from_list(values)))
```
solution:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next


def from_list(values):
    head = None
    for x in reversed(values):
        head = Node(x, head)
    return head


def to_list(head):
    values = []
    while head is not None:
        values.append(head.val)
        head = head.next
    return values


def delete_duplicates(head):
    dummy = Node(0, head)
    prev = dummy
    cur = head
    while cur is not None:
        if cur.next is not None and cur.next.val == cur.val:
            while cur.next is not None and cur.next.val == cur.val:
                cur = cur.next
            prev.next = cur.next
        else:
            prev = cur
        cur = cur.next
    return dummy.next


def run(values):
    return to_list(delete_duplicates(from_list(values)))
```
tests:
- run([1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5])
- run([1, 1, 1, 2, 3])
- run([])
- run([1, 1])
- run([1, 2, 2])
- (lambda t: [len(t), t[:6], t[-6:], sum(t)])(run(sorted(i * 37 % 201 - 100 for i in range(300))))
fail[2]: The run of three 1s starts at the head: the dummy links past all of them, to 2.
fail[3]: An empty list stays empty.
fail[4]: Both nodes are copies of 1, so nothing is left.
fail[5]: The run of 2s is at the end, so the 1 must link to None.
fail[6]: 300 sorted values from −100 to 100, of which 99 appear twice.
edges:
- an empty list :: len(values) == 0 :: run([])
- the head is a duplicate :: len(values) > 1 and values[0] == values[1] :: run([1, 1, 2])
- the head is kept :: len(values) > 0 and (len(values) == 1 or values[0] != values[1]) :: run([1, 2, 2])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(1)
complexity_note: n is the number of nodes.
why_time: cur walks the list once; every node is passed once, inside a run of copies or not.
why_space: A dummy and two pointers.
hint: The starter sets up dummy, prev = dummy and cur = head.
hint: While cur isn't None: if cur.next isn't None and `cur.next.val == cur.val`, move cur on while the next value is still the same, then `prev.next = cur.next`.
hint: Otherwise `prev = cur`. In both cases, finish with `cur = cur.next`.
right: Because equal values are neighbours, each run of copies can be skipped as a whole, and prev only moves onto nodes known to be unique, so one walk removes every copy.
wrong: Walk prev from a dummy, with cur ahead of it. When cur starts a run of equal values, skip to the run's last node and link prev past it; otherwise move prev to cur. Then move cur on.

